 "text": "Same thousand dollars, same S&P 500, one friend ended with ten times as much. Wait, how is that possible? They all bought in at the start of two thousand and six. Then the two thousand and eight crash cut each thousand to about seven hundred and seventy. Jay sells at year-end and hides the cash in a shoebox. Maya sells too, then buys back a year later after missing a twenty-six percent rebound. Sam stays invested and lets his dividends reinvest. At first, Jay feels smart because shoebox money can't crash. By the end of twenty twenty-five, Jay has seven hundred and seventy, Maya about sixty-four hundred, and Sam about eight thousand. Selling in a crash is the shoebox mistake, and coming back just one year late cost Maya about seventeen hundred. So if your money crashed tomorrow, would you sell, wait, or hold?",
